sentences.t:
Respuso Búcar al Çid: “cofonda Dios tal amiztad!
Espada tienes en mano e veot aguijar;
así commo semeja, en mi la quieres ensayar.

sentences.l:
Replied Búcar to-the Çid: “may-confound God such friendship!
Sword you-have in hand and I-see-you spur;
so as it-seems, on me it you-wish to-try.

sentences.t:
Alegrós mio Çid fermoso sonrrisando:
“¿Venides, mios yernos, mios fijos sodes amos!
Sé que de lidiar bien sodes pagados;

sentences.l:
Gladdened-himself my Çid warmly smiling:
“Are-you-coming, my sons-in-law, my sons you-are both!
I-know that of fighting well you-are satisfied;

sentences.t:
Minaya Álbar Fáñez essora es llegado,
el escudo trae al cuello e todo espad[ad]o;
de los colpes de las lanças non avie recabdo;

sentences.l:
Minaya Albar Fáñez then is arrived,
the shield he-brings at-the neck and all sword-hacked;
of the blows of the lances not there-was reckoning;

sentences.t:
aquellos que gelos dieran non gelo avien logrado.
Por el cobdo ayuso la sangre destellando;
de veínte arriba ha moros matado;

sentences.l:
those who to-him-them had-given not to-him-it had profited.
Through the elbow down the blood dripping;
from twenty up he-has Moors killed;

sentences.t:
Todos l[os] gana[dos] a Valençia son llegad[os];
alegre es mio Çid con todos [sos vassallos],
que a la raçión ca[di]e de plata seys çientos marcos.

sentences.l:
All the gain[s] to Valençia are arrived;
glad is my Çid with all [his vassals],
for to the share fell of silver six hundred marks.

The sons-in-law of my Çid when this wealth they-took
from-this rout, that it they-held in their safe-keeping,
they-thought that in [their] days never they-would-be in-want.
